whether you're debugging a specific  application or monitoring multiple  applications across several production  clusters  you need logs i'm here today to show you  how to use cloud logging to easily find  and analyze the logs you need from your  applications running on gke  this is the stackdoctor  [Music]  cloud logging is deeply integrated into  gke that means that collecting  infrastructure and application logs  is easy gke deploys a per node logging  agent then automatically collects  standard output and error logs  it then sends logs to the large router  which sends them to cloud logging and  any of the logging sync destinations  that you have configured  you can start viewing your logs as soon  as your application is deployed  the easiest place to find the logs is in  the logs viewer in the cloud console  you can get there by selecting that  option from the products menu  another way to access logs just for your  service quickly is from the gke workload  screen  just select your deployment and then  click container logs  either option brings you to the logs  viewer note that we're looking at the  log viewer preview  you may be more familiar with the  classic logs viewer which has less  functionality  the new logs viewer has four main areas  the query builder at the top  the histogram below that the logs field  explorer on the left  and the query results on the right the  query builder is at the top of the  screen  this is how you create and run queries  to retrieve the logs that you want  you can also add query parameters  directly and use the autocomplete to  select available fields  the logs fields explorer on the left is  there so you can easily find logs by the  field and value in the log payload  one example would be to look at all the  pods in the namespace  since i'm looking at logs for a specific  service already i can click on error in  the severity field to find just the  error logs for that service  let's do this now the histogram at the  top of the screen shows the frequency  and relative volume of the errors you're  seeing  you can also use the histogram to select  specific time ranges to search for  the query results show the actual log  entries that match our filters  you can expand any of the entries in the  results to see the full log payload i'm  seeing an authentication error in my  logs  finally you can click on any of the  payload fields and select show  matching entries to see all log entries  that contain this message if you want to  see where else you may be getting a  particular error  you've now learned how logs are ingested  from your gke workloads into cloud  logging  and how to use the logs viewer and its  field explorer and histogram features  to find exactly what you're looking for  i hope this helps you to debug and  troubleshoot your own workloads on gke  thanks for watching and come back soon  for more this is the snack doctor  stay healthy out there
 

